On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:39:34 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> The spec commit is way too big. Besides, I don't want spec-only
> commits. The spec changes should be committed along with the code.

Agreed. The spec-only patches are gone, and each spec change now sits
in the patch whose code it describes.

> Reroll this series. It should be a series of atomic commits, each
> changing one distinct unit of behavior, with the specs included.

Done, as "[PATCH v13 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's
mail hub" [1]. It applies to trunk with git am. It has eight patches,
one step each: the list in INBOX, Dovecot as the only writer, the
two-way sync, your login, the import, OpenSMTPD, the lists.p7x.dev
addresses, and Next and Later. Each one carries its own spec text,
README, and code.

Two choices you may want to check:

- The spec now covers only what the series builds. v12's client
  sections (your notmuch and Emacs, folder tags, git send-email, and
  retiring the mail bookmark) had no code here. I left them out, to
  come back with the series that builds the clients. That also cuts
  spec.org from 506 lines to 398.
- Patches 2 and 6 are still large. Patch 2 changes who owns the
  maildir, and patch 6 swaps one SMTP server for another on the same
  port. Neither can be split without leaving a jail that does not
  work. The cover letter has the details.

The code at the end of the series is identical to v12's. Along the
way I found that v12 left lab-claude's client pulling the old lists
mailbox, so v13 updates it in patches 1 and 6.

Thanks for the review.

